(Bug #50 was never print-specific - the chain-continuation guard is shared,
 so a bare `otherwise` after any non-append base action was refused.
 `increment` is the second base action the fix has to carry. Each branch
 counts into its own tally so the test says WHICH branch ran, not merely
 that something did: an `otherwise` supplies the chain's default, so the
 base action itself never runs once one is present, and `tally` stays 1.)

a number called tally is 1.
a number called highs is 0.
a number called lows is 0.
increment tally, but if tally is greater than 50 increment highs, otherwise increment lows.
increment tally, but if tally is greater than 0 increment highs, otherwise increment lows.
print tally.
print highs.
print lows.
